'''Collective intelligence''' is the capacity of a group or coordinated system to solve problems, make judgments or accomplish intellectual tasks using contributions from multiple participants.

== Aggregation versus interaction ==
For n unbiased estimates of a quantity with equal error variance σ² and pairwise correlation ρ, the variance of their mean is <math>\sigma^2(1+(n-1)\rho)/n</math>. Independent errors average out; shared bias or correlated errors do not. This is a model of aggregation rather than all collective intelligence. Interactive groups may create new representations, allocate tasks, negotiate and adapt to feedback.

== Empirical evidence ==
Woolley et al. (2010) reported a common group-performance factor across tasks in small human groups. Riedl et al. (2021) evaluated cross-task predictive performance across multiple datasets. Other studies emphasize the role of member abilities and task composition; the construct and measurement assumptions remain debated. Lorenz et al. (2011) experimentally demonstrated that social influence can reduce diversity without improving aggregate accuracy. These are bounded empirical findings, not proof of a unitary collective mind.

== Engineering and measurement ==
Evaluate groups against individual and noninteractive aggregation baselines matched for time and resources. Record independence of evidence, task allocation, communication costs, dissent and error correlations. For artificial agents, ablate coordination mechanisms and measure correctness and resource cost on held-out tasks. Shared training data may create correlated errors. Performance does not demonstrate subjective experience or general intelligence beyond the evaluated task distribution.

